<Base title="Design" current="/design" description="Where Koudelka came from and how it was designed: the idea, the setting, the cast, the combat and the sound.">
  <div class="page ed">
    <p class="mono">Design notes</p>
    <h1>Why it feels like this</h1>
    <p>Koudelka is a game about a single night in a single building. Almost everything that makes it memorable was a decision made to fit that idea, and a few were decisions the director would later regret. These notes follow the game from its origin to its sound.</p>

    <Plate slot="design-hero" class="pic-hero" />

    <h2>Where it came from</h2>
    <Plate slot="design-kikuta" class="pic-small" />
    <p>Hiroki Kikuta studied religion, philosophy and cultural anthropology, drew manga, scored anime, and joined Square in 1991, where he wrote the music for <em>Secret of Mana</em> and <em>Trials of Mana</em>. He wanted to direct. He left, founded Sacnoth in 1997 with money from SNK and set out to make a mature, dark horror role-playing game, because he found the genre disjointed, juvenile and stagnant. His belief was that the best projects have only a few people making the key decisions. Three months went on research alone, and he says he read over a hundred books, many of them on British history. The scale was set early, and for practical reasons: a small team and a deadline meant one place and one night, so the game would not have to build a world, only a house.</p>

    <h2>The place</h2>
    <Plate slot="design-stdavids" />
    <Plate slot="design-pembrokeshire" />
    <p>Kikuta put the monastery on the Welsh coast because it faced Ireland across the sea. Because Japan had few sources on British architecture, he and several of the team travelled to Pembrokeshire. The cliffs, the ancient ruins and the weather shaped the setting, and the visit to St Davids Cathedral gave them decorative features and individual room layouts, though not the plan of the complex. He chose 1898 on purpose: a moment when belief in spirits and faith in machinery lived uneasily side by side. The art director, Matsuzo Machida, set himself a difficult task: to fit the variety of a role-playing game's whole world map into one building.</p>
    <p>The place is also layered. It is a Celtic sacred site, a monastery, a prison, a house and, by the end, a ruin, and the game lets you feel each of those under your feet. <a href="/walkthrough">The walkthrough</a> follows that building room by room.</p>

    <h2>What it was built from</h2>
    <Plate slot="design-directors-cut" />
    <p>The strongest influence on tone was <em>Carnacki, the Ghost-Finder</em>, William Hope Hodgson’s occult-detective stories, in which a mystery is either genuinely supernatural or a trick played by people who want others to believe it is. Add the darker side of medieval history, the proto-science of alchemy, Lovecraft, <em>The Name of the Rose</em> and the Irish fantasy writer Lord Dunsany, whose name the studio borrowed and whose real life became one of the characters’. The look of the game owes a great deal to three photographers: Bob Carlos Clarke, Jan Saudek and Holly Warburton. History comes in too: a real steamer disaster of 1878, a real thirteenth-century friar and a real noblewoman of the early eighteenth century are all turned into fiction.</p>

    <h2>The cast</h2>
    <Plate slot="design-sketches" />
    <Plate slot="design-mocap" />
    <p>Yūji Iwahara drew more than a hundred sketches for the characters. He modelled Koudelka on two film heroines, Natalie Portman in <em>Léon</em> and Winona Ryder in <em>Alien Resurrection</em>, to give her an air of confidence and a sense of vulnerability at once. Her background as an exile came from Kikuta’s wish for a lonely, isolated lead. Because the console had harsh polygon and memory limits her design was simplified, and the CG model by Nobuji Watanabe went through several drafts. The name came from the photographer Josef Koudelka, whose sound the director liked, with no obvious origin attached.</p>
    <p>The voices are in English throughout, even in Japan, where the game was subtitled. Kikuta wanted motion capture to carry the drama and to cut the labour of hand-animating more than an hour of cut-scenes, but the Japanese equipment could capture one actor at a time. A former Square colleague pointed him to Future Light, a Santa Monica studio that had pioneered real-time wireless capture of several actors at once, with their dialogue recorded in the same take. More than a hundred actors answered the casting call. The scenes were rehearsed in a hotel function room and recorded over four days in a sound studio converted from a warehouse, with bare sets made of whatever was lying around, and the recording was often halted by outside noise. Kikuta left the directing to David Walding so the English performances would feel natural, and says that for one famous scene between Koudelka and Edward he filmed a ten-minute single take ten times, in January 1999. <a href="/people">Meet them.</a></p>

    <h2>How it plays</h2>
    <p>Exploration uses 3D models on pre-rendered backgrounds, with fixed cameras and tank-style controls, the way <em>Resident Evil</em> did. Combat is turn-based, on a grid, with random encounters, and weapons break with use. It was not what the director wanted: citing <em>Resident Evil</em> and survival horror, he had imagined action-based, real-time fights, but his staff were set on something closer to the Japanese role-playing games Square was making, and the result was a compromise. He said later that he did not have enough say over the design. The art director added that battles were made easy on purpose, so adults who rarely played games could reach the ending.</p>
    <p>Reviews at the time agreed about the cost: the pacing was slow and the fights slower. They also agreed about the reward: the atmosphere, the cut-scenes and the acting.</p>

    <h2>The sound</h2>
    <Plate slot="design-ost-cover" class="pic-small" />
    <p>Kikuta composed the score himself, working alone, in about six months, mostly with a sequencer on an Atari. He called it the easiest part of the project. The result has less music than most role-playing games: ambient beds, and short pieces attached to scenes, instead of long loops. With help from the arranger Nick Ingman, the opening, “Requiem”, is sung by the soprano Catherine Bott. The closing, “Ubi Caritas et amor”, is a sacred choral work by Maurice Duruflé, sung by a London school choir, and not Kikuta’s own. The soundtrack was released in December 1999 with 34 tracks, the last three of them live recordings of battle themes.</p>

    <h2>What was never made</h2>
    <p>Kikuta had ideas for sequels. The second would have been set in 1900, with Koudelka drawn to the Paris World’s Fair, then to the new Métro and the catacombs beneath it (he later mentioned Nikola Tesla and the novelist Natsume Sōseki as characters she would meet). The third, Chicago in 1973, follows her grandson Rodmell, an anthropologist, among soldiers returned from Vietnam who may be possessed, with Roger Bacon back in a modern suit. The fourth is Japan in 1984, a curse laid by an onmyōji, and the Asuka stones. None was made. Another studio’s series, <em>Shadow Hearts</em>, picked up the story afterwards, and its director was Koudelka’s art director. <a href="/archive/legacy">What came next.</a></p>


